Stage 1 — New feature development
The scenario

A Zepto product manager raises a requirement. "We want to show personalised health product recommendations based on past purchases. If someone bought protein powder last month, show them fitness supplements."

A developer picks up the ticket. They build the feature. They write a recommendation engine that reads order history, identifies health-related products, and uses purchase patterns to personalise suggestions.

They test it. It works. They raise a pull request.

What the developer did not think about

Purchase history of health products is health-adjacent data. Under DPDPA, using someone's health-related purchase behaviour to profile them for targeted recommendations requires explicit consent for that specific purpose.

Zepto's original consent when the user signed up said "we collect your data to process orders and improve our service." It did not say "we will analyse your health product purchases to profile you for targeted health recommendations."

That is a purpose limitation violation. Section 6 of DPDPA. The data was collected for delivery. It is now being used for profiling. Different purpose. New consent required.

The developer did not know this. The product manager did not think about it. The code reviewer did not catch it.

Where dpdpa-guard runs

