Stage 3 — Real-time data pipeline protection
The scenario

Zepto's data team runs nightly ETL pipelines. Raw transactional data flows from their production database into their data warehouse for analytics. Data scientists use the warehouse to analyse order patterns, forecast demand, and build ML models.

The problem nobody noticed

The ETL pipeline copies data as-is from production to the warehouse. The warehouse has 200 people with access. Most of them are analysts and data scientists who have no need to see actual customer names, phone numbers, or addresses. They just need aggregate patterns.

But the raw data — including names, phones, addresses, and order history — sits in the warehouse visible to all 200 people. This violates DPDPA's data minimisation principle and creates massive breach exposure.

Where dpdpa-guard runs

dpdpa-guard is added as a transformation step inside the ETL pipeline. Before data moves from production to the warehouse, every row passes through dpdpa-guard.

For each row it detects PII fields. For each detected field it applies the anonymisation strategy from the knowledge engine:

Customer name → encrypted (analysts do not need real names, but customer service might need to retrieve them)
Phone number → hashed (analysts just need to count unique users, not call them)
Delivery address → replaced with area-level location only (street address replaced with neighbourhood — enough for demand forecasting, not enough to identify an individual)
Order items → passed through unchanged (not PII, needed for analysis)
Payment UPI ID → replaced entirely (no analytical value whatsoever)

The data warehouse now contains analytically useful data with zero raw PII. Data scientists can do their work. A breach of the warehouse exposes nothing meaningful. 200 people can access it safely.

The developer who built the ETL pipeline added three lines of code to integrate dpdpa-guard. They did not make 47 individual decisions about how to handle each field. dpdpa-guard made those decisions based on the DPDPA knowledge engine. The developer just called the function.

Stage 4 — User right to erasure
The scenario

A Zepto user exercises their right under DPDPA Section 13 — the right to erasure. They send a deletion request. Under DPDPA, Zepto must delete all their personal data within a reasonable time.

The problem

Zepto's data lives in eleven different places. Main production database. Data warehouse. Customer support system. Email marketing platform. Push notification service. Fraud detection system. Delivery partner app. Cold storage backups. Analytics events database. ML training datasets. Log files.

Finding and deleting one person's data from all eleven systems manually is a multi-day engineering task. At scale — if hundreds of users request erasure — it becomes impossible to do manually.

Where dpdpa-guard runs

dpdpa-guard's deletion scheduler module maintains a data map — it knows which systems contain which types of personal data and how each system stores user identifiers.

When a deletion request comes in for user ID 48291:

dpdpa-guard queries its data map. It identifies all eleven systems that contain data linked to this user. It generates deletion jobs for each system. It tracks completion. It generates a Certificate of Erasure — a tamper-evident document proving that the deletion happened, when it happened, and which systems were cleared.

The compliance officer has proof. The user gets confirmation. The whole process takes minutes instead of days. And it is auditable — if a regulator asks for proof that Zepto honoured deletion requests, the certificates exist.

What the Zepto engineering team actually did

To integrate dpdpa-guard into all four stages above, their developers did four things:

